<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hogmanay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hogmanay is the Scots word for the last day of the old year and for the celebration of the New Year in the Scottish manner. The etymology of the word is obscure, the main modern theories deriving it from a French, Norse or Gaelic root; it is first recorded in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> entry of 1443 in the West Riding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> and in Scots in 1604, in the records of Elgin. Its roots are speculated to reach back to the Norse celebration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a> and the Gaelic Samhain. The most widespread custom is first-footing, in which the first person to cross a friend's or neighbour's threshold after midnight brings symbolic gifts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, shortbread, whisky and black bun.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hogmanay.jpg" alt="Hogmanay" width="330" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robbie Shade · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EdinburghNYE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Singing it at <b>Hogmanay</b>, the Scottish New Year, quickly became a custom in Scotland and spread across the British Isles, and emigrants from Scotland, England, Wales and Ireland carried it around the world.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a></li></ul></section>
